From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hobbyquaker <hobbyquaker@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 07:12:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc_otg: bounce IN transfers whose length is not a
 multiple of 4

The DWC OTG core in BCM2835 through BCM2712 only does DMA writes of
whole words. An IN transfer into a buffer whose length is not a
multiple of 4 therefore gets up to 3 bytes written past the end of the
buffer. dwc_otg bounces only buffers whose start is not word aligned,
so an aligned buffer of odd length goes straight to the DMA engine.

The most visible case is usb_get_device_descriptor(): it allocates
exactly sizeof(struct usb_device_descriptor), 18 bytes, and the core
writes 20. kmalloc's size classes usually hide the two bytes in the
object's slack, but with CONFIG_KFENCE a sampled allocation sits at the
end of a page and the overrun is reported, on a Raspberry Pi 3 B while
its LAN9514 enumerates:

  BUG: KFENCE: memory corruption in hub_port_init+0x5b0/0xcf0

with the two bytes after an 18-byte kmalloc-64 object from
usb_get_device_descriptor() overwritten.

Extend the existing non-dword-aligned path in assign_and_init_hc() to
IN transfers whose remaining length is not a multiple of 4, for the
non-isochronous and the isochronous (per frame) cases. The align buffer
is at least as large as the transfer, and the completion handlers
already copy back only the bytes actually received, so the URB's
buffer is never written past its length. The FIQ FSM programs
hc->align_buff for non-periodic splits and for interrupt splits sent
as control transfers, and completes those through the same handlers;
periodic split IN transfers use the FIQ's own bounce buffers as before,
and so do high-speed isochronous transfers run by the FIQ FSM, which
use the URB's buffer directly (unchanged).

The dwc2 driver in the Raspberry Pi tree bounces the same case since
commit 279fbca16a ("drivers: dwc2: better handle hardware length &
alignment issues").
---
 drivers/usb/host/dwc_otg/dwc_otg_hcd.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/host/dwc_otg/dwc_otg_hcd.c b/drivers/usb/host/dwc_otg/dwc_otg_hcd.c
index 9e05e07..cdaa3f9 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/host/dwc_otg/dwc_otg_hcd.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/dwc_otg/dwc_otg_hcd.c
@@ -1280,6 +1280,19 @@ static void assign_and_init_hc(dwc_otg_hcd_t * hcd, dwc_otg_qh_t * qh)
 	hc->xfer_len = urb->length - urb->actual_length;
 	hc->xfer_count = 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * Hardware bug: the core only does DMA writes of whole words, so an
+	 * IN transfer into a buffer whose length is not a multiple of 4 gets
+	 * up to 3 bytes written past the end of the buffer. Bounce such
+	 * transfers through the align buffer; the completion copies back
+	 * only the bytes received. The control SETUP and STATUS phases below
+	 * clear ptr again, and isochronous transfers set it per frame.
+	 */
+	if (hcd->core_if->dma_enable && !hcd->core_if->dma_desc_enable &&
+	    hc->ep_is_in && (hc->xfer_len & 0x3) && urb->buf) {
+		ptr = (uint8_t *) urb->buf + urb->actual_length;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * Set the split attributes
 	 */
@@ -1400,8 +1413,9 @@ static void assign_and_init_hc(dwc_otg_hcd_t * hcd, dwc_otg_qh_t * qh)
 			hc->xfer_len =
 			    frame_desc->length - qtd->isoc_split_offset;
 
-			/* For non-dword aligned buffers */
-			if (((unsigned long)hc->xfer_buff & 0x3)
+			/* For non-dword aligned buffers and odd-length IN frames */
+			if ((((unsigned long)hc->xfer_buff & 0x3) ||
+			     (hc->ep_is_in && (hc->xfer_len & 0x3) && urb->buf))
 			    && hcd->core_if->dma_enable) {
 				ptr =
 				    (uint8_t *) urb->buf + frame_desc->offset +
